IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `faa-dates`
IMPORT `faa-relative`

-- Fatal Accidents Act 1959 (WA) -- worked cases, from the seat of the relative of the person who died.
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (mrdoc_49105), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario life raised this Act; the
-- facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every finding of a court is given as a fact.
--
-- A breached or residual DEONTIC cannot be compared with EQUALS on the stable l4, so the s 9 rule is asserted through
-- CONSIDER ... WHEN FULFILLED, and one residual is also #TRACEd with an "-- expect:" line.

-- HYPOTHETICAL: Daniel is killed on 10 January 2026 when a truck runs a red light. The six months of s 9(1) end on
-- 10 July 2026.
`Daniel died` MEANS `the day` 10 1 2026
`by birth` MEANS `by birth or marriage, with no adoption and no parent and child unmarried to each other along the way`
`any date` MEANS `the day` 1 1 2000

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Schedule 2 and s 3: am I a relative. Arguments: relationship, date of death, date began living together, living together
-- immediately before the death, care in place immediately before the death, legally obliged to provide, how traced,
-- parentage admitted or established in time, born after the death.

-- Mia, Daniel's wife (separated, still married): (a)(i).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `spouse (married to the deceased when they died, even if separated)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's spouse immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(i))`
-- A de facto partner who moved in on 10 January 2024: exactly 2 years on the day of the death, so "at least 2 years". The last day.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` `Daniel died` (`the day` 10 1 2024) TRUE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
-- One day short: moved in on 11 January 2024.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` `Daniel died` (`the day` 11 1 2024) TRUE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: a de facto partner is a relative only if living with the deceased on that basis immediately before the death and for at least 2 years (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
-- Ten years together, but they had stopped living together before the death.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 1 2016) FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: a de facto partner is a relative only if living with the deceased on that basis immediately before the death and for at least 2 years (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
-- The way a blood relationship is traced has nothing to do with a de facto partner.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 1 2016) TRUE FALSE FALSE `to the birth family of a person who was adopted out` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
-- 29 February: moved in 29 February 2024; two years on is 28 February 2026 (no 29th). Death on 28 February 2026: yes; on 27 February 2026: no.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` (`the day` 28 2 2026) (`the day` 29 2 2024) TRUE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was the deceased's de facto partner, living with them on that basis for at least 2 years immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `de facto partner` (`the day` 27 2 2026) (`the day` 29 2 2024) TRUE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: a de facto partner is a relative only if living with the deceased on that basis immediately before the death and for at least 2 years (Sch 2 cl 1(a)(ii))`
-- A former wife with a spousal maintenance order: (g). Without one: no.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `former spouse or former de facto partner` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE TRUE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I am a former spouse or former de facto partner whom the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `former spouse or former de facto partner` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: a former spouse or former de facto partner is a relative only if the deceased was legally obliged, immediately before the death, to provide for them financially (Sch 2 cl 1(g))`
-- Daniel was raising his niece as his own: (d). The uncle who raised Daniel: (e). Care that had ended before the death: no.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `a person the deceased was bringing up as their own child (the deceased stood in loco parentis to me)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE TRUE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: the deceased stood in loco parentis to me immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `a person who was bringing up the deceased as their own child (I stood in loco parentis to the deceased)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE TRUE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I stood in loco parentis to the deceased immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(e))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `a person the deceased was bringing up as their own child (the deceased stood in loco parentis to me)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: standing in loco parentis counts only if it held immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d), (e))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `a person who was bringing up the deceased as their own child (I stood in loco parentis to the deceased)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: standing in loco parentis counts only if it held immediately before the death (Sch 2 cl 1(d), (e))`
-- Parents, grandparents, step parents: (b).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `parent` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `grandparent` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `step parent` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a parent, grandparent or step parent of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(b))`
-- Children, grandchildren, stepchildren: (c).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `grandson or granddaughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `stepson or stepdaughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
-- Daniel's daughter, born in March 2026 after his death: s 3(4).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I am a child of the deceased born alive after the death, and treated as born before it (s 3(4), Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
-- A grandchild born after the death: s 3(4) speaks only of a child of the deceased.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `grandson or granddaughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `section 3(4) treats only a child of the deceased born after the death as born before it; this Act does not say whether a grandchild or other relative born after the death counts`
-- Brothers and sisters, whole and half: (f).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `brother or sister` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(f))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `half-brother or half-sister` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a brother, sister, half-brother or half-sister of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(f))`
-- An aunt, a fiance: not listed (a "means" definition).
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: Schedule 2 does not list my relationship to the deceased`
-- s 3(2)(a): Daniel's adopted son is his child.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through an adoption, to the adoptive family` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
-- Daniel's birth son, adopted out as a baby: the Act does not say.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `to the birth family of a person who was adopted out` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `this Act does not say whether an adopted person stays related to their birth family (s 3(2)(a) speaks only of the adopters; see the Adoption Act 1994)`
-- s 6(3)(a): Daniel's son, born to parents not married to each other; Daniel acknowledged him during his life: recognised. Never admitted or established: not.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the child` TRUE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the child` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: my relationship runs through a parent and a child whose parents were not married, and parentage was not admitted or established in the lifetime section 6(3) requires`
-- s 6(3)(b): Daniel's father, not married to his mother, who never admitted parentage in Daniel's lifetime: not recognised.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `parent` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: my relationship runs through a parent and a child whose parents were not married, and parentage was not admitted or established in the lifetime section 6(3) requires`
-- s 6(3) reaches a relationship traced through it: a half-sister through Daniel's unacknowledged father: not recognised.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `half-brother or half-sister` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through a parent and a child whose parents were not married to each other, my claim being by or through the parent` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: my relationship runs through a parent and a child whose parents were not married, and parentage was not admitted or established in the lifetime section 6(3) requires`
-- s 6(3a): a parentage under the Artificial Conception Act 1985 needs no admission.
#ASSERT `Am I a relative question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `through a parentage established by the Artificial Conception Act 1985` FALSE FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: I was a son, daughter, grandson, granddaughter, stepson or stepdaughter of the deceased (Sch 2 cl 1(c))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 4: does the death give a claim. Arguments: wrongful act, could have sued, own time expired, what the deceased knew.
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `yes: the person who would have been liable had the deceased lived is liable to an action for damages for the death, even if the death was caused by a crime, and the Crown is bound (s 4(1), s 11)`
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `no: section 4(1) applies only where the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` FALSE TRUE TRUE `did not know the physical cause of the injury, and it was reasonable not to know it` EQUALS `no: section 4(1) applies only where the death was caused by a wrongful act, neglect or default`
-- Daniel was himself wholly to blame (no one else's act could have been sued on).
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE FALSE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `no: section 4(1) applies only where the deceased, had they lived, could have sued and recovered damages for it`
-- HYPOTHETICAL: a worker dies of mesothelioma from asbestos exposure decades earlier; his own time had run.
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `no: the deceased's own time limit had expired before the death, and section 4(2) does not treat the deceased as entitled to sue on these facts`
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE `did not know the physical cause of the injury, and it was reasonable not to know it` EQUALS `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE `knew the physical cause, but not that the injury was due to someone's conduct, and it was reasonable not to know that` EQUALS `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`
#ASSERT `Does the death give a claim question` TRUE TRUE TRUE `knew the cause and that someone's conduct caused it, but after reasonable enquiry could not find out who` EQUALS `yes: although the deceased's own time limit had expired, the deceased is taken to have been entitled to sue (s 4(2)), so the person who would have been liable is liable to an action for damages (s 4(1))`

-- s 6(1A) with Sch 2 and s 4: can a claim be made for my benefit.
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `spouse (married to the deceased when they died, even if separated)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `answered` `yes: the death gives an action for damages, and an action under this Act is brought for the benefit of the relatives, of whom I am one (s 4, s 6(1A))`
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `some other relationship (for example aunt, uncle, niece, nephew, cousin, fiance or friend)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `answered` `no: an action under this Act is for the benefit of the relatives of the deceased only, and I am not one (s 6(1A), Sch 2)`
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `de facto partner` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 6 2025) TRUE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `answered` `no: an action under this Act is for the benefit of the relatives of the deceased only, and I am not one (s 6(1A), Sch 2)`
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `spouse (married to the deceased when they died, even if separated)` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `answered` `no: the death does not give an action under section 4 on these facts`
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `son or daughter` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `to the birth family of a person who was adopted out` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE `none of the section 4(2) circumstances` EQUALS `not covered` `this Act does not say whether an adopted person stays related to their birth family (s 3(2)(a) speaks only of the adopters; see the Adoption Act 1994)`
#ASSERT `Can a claim be made for my benefit question` `brother or sister` `Daniel died` `any date` FALSE FALSE FALSE `by birth` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE `knew the cause and that someone's conduct caused it, but after reasonable enquiry could not find out who` EQUALS `answered` `yes: the death gives an action for damages, and an action under this Act is brought for the benefit of the relatives, of whom I am one (s 4, s 6(1A))`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- ss 6(1B), 7, 9: can I bring the action myself. Arguments: date of death, date asked about, executor exists, who has
-- brought it, date brought, accrued before 15 November 2005.
-- No executor or administrator: a relative may bring it at once.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 15 1 2026) FALSE `no one yet` `any date` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: there is no executor or administrator, so any one or more of the relatives may bring the action, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))`
-- An executor, no action yet: on 10 July 2026, the last day of the 6 months, not yet; on 11 July 2026, yes.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 10 7 2026) TRUE `no one yet` `any date` FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`not yet: the executor or administrator brings the action (s 6(1B)); if it is not brought within 6 months after the death, I may bring it (s 9(1))` (`the day` 10 7 2026))
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 11 7 2026) TRUE `no one yet` `any date` FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: the executor or administrator did not bring the action within the 6 months after the death, so any one or more of the relatives may bring it, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))` (`the day` 10 7 2026))
-- The executor sued on 10 July 2026, the last day: the action is for all the relatives.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 9 2026) TRUE `the executor or administrator` (`the day` 10 7 2026) FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no need: the executor or administrator brought the action within the 6 months, and it is for the benefit of the relatives, me included (s 6(1A), (1B)); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
-- The executor sued late, on 11 July 2026: s 9(1) opened to the relatives, but one action already lies (s 7).
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 9 2026) TRUE `the executor or administrator` (`the day` 11 7 2026) FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: one action has already been brought, and no more than one action lies for the same subject matter of complaint (s 7); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
-- Daniel's brother has already sued under s 9 (no executor): Mia cannot bring a second action.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` `Daniel died` (`the day` 1 9 2026) FALSE `a relative, or some relatives (s 9)` (`the day` 1 3 2026) FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: one action has already been brought, and no more than one action lies for the same subject matter of complaint (s 7); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
-- The same, for a death in 2004: the former s 7 governs, and is not deposited.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` (`the day` 1 6 2004) (`the day` 1 9 2005) FALSE `a relative, or some relatives (s 9)` (`the day` 1 3 2005) TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `the cause of action accrued before 15 November 2005, so the former section 7 applies to it (Limitation Legislation Amendment and Repeal Act 2005 s 13(2)), and its text is not deposited`
-- An executor's action within 6 months of a 2004 death is still for the relatives (s 6(1A), (1B) apply whatever s 7 said).
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` (`the day` 1 6 2004) (`the day` 1 9 2005) TRUE `the executor or administrator` (`the day` 1 10 2004) TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no need: the executor or administrator brought the action within the 6 months, and it is for the benefit of the relatives, me included (s 6(1A), (1B)); if I am not named in it, see section 9A`
-- A death on 31 August 2025: no 31 February, so the 6 months end on 28 February 2026.
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` (`the day` 31 8 2025) (`the day` 28 2 2026) TRUE `no one yet` `any date` FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`not yet: the executor or administrator brings the action (s 6(1B)); if it is not brought within 6 months after the death, I may bring it (s 9(1))` (`the day` 28 2 2026))
#ASSERT `Can I bring the action myself question` (`the day` 31 8 2025) (`the day` 1 3 2026) TRUE `no one yet` `any date` FALSE EQUALS `answered` (`yes: the executor or administrator did not bring the action within the 6 months after the death, so any one or more of the relatives may bring it, for the benefit of those who bring it (s 9(1), (2))` (`the day` 28 2 2026))

-- The s 9 rule itself, day 0 being the death. From 10 January 2026 the 6 months run 181 days, to 10 July 2026.
#ASSERT `days from` `Daniel died` `to` (`the last day of the 6 months after` `Daniel died`) EQUALS 181
-- The executor sues on day 181: fulfilled.
`the executor sues on the last day` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for a death on` `Daniel died`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` 181))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `the executor sues on the last day` EQUALS TRUE
-- The executor sues on day 182: too late to be the executor's turn; the relatives' permission is open, and unused.
`the executor sues a day late` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for a death on` `Daniel died`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` 182))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `the executor sues a day late` EQUALS FALSE
-- After that, Mia sues on day 190: the relatives' permission used.
`the executor misses the 6 months and I sue` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for a death on` `Daniel died`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `the executor or administrator` `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` 182), (EVENT `I, a relative for whose benefit the action might be brought` `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` 190))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `the executor misses the 6 months and I sue` EQUALS TRUE
-- Mia sues on day 100, inside the executor's 6 months: that does not count as the s 9 action.
`I sue inside the executor's 6 months` MEANS
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE (`the s 9 rule for a death on` `Daniel died`) 0 (LIST (EVENT `I, a relative for whose benefit the action might be brought` `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` 100))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE
#ASSERT `I sue inside the executor's 6 months` EQUALS FALSE
#TRACE `the s 9 rule for a death on` `Daniel died` AT 0 WITH
    PARTY `the executor or administrator` DOES `bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act` AT 182
-- expect: not BREACH: the residual permission, PARTY I, a relative ... MAY bring the action under the Fatal Accidents Act

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 8: particulars.
#ASSERT `What must I give the defendant question` TRUE EQUALS `I must deliver to the defendant or the defendant's solicitor full particulars of every person for whom and on whose behalf the action is brought, and of the nature of the claim for damages (s 8, s 9(2))`
#ASSERT `What must I give the defendant question` FALSE EQUALS `nothing under section 8: the duty to deliver particulars is the plaintiff's`

-- s 9A(1): added to the action. Arguments: action commenced, my name included, court satisfied.
#ASSERT `Can I be added to the action question` FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: section 9A(1) applies only once an action under this Act has been commenced (see whether I can bring it, s 9)`
#ASSERT `Can I be added to the action question` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no need: my name is already among those for whose benefit the action is stated to be brought`
-- Daniel's posthumous daughter, left out of the executor's statement of claim.
#ASSERT `Can I be added to the action question` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `possibly: the court may, on my application or of its own motion, order the action to proceed as if my name had been included (s 9A(1)); it has the power, not a duty`
#ASSERT `Can I be added to the action question` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: section 9A(1) lets the court add me only if it is satisfied my name should have been included`
-- s 9A(2): separate representation.
#ASSERT `Can I have my own lawyer question` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `possibly: the court may order that I be separately represented (s 9A(2)); it is the court's choice`
#ASSERT `Can I have my own lawyer question` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `section 9A(2) speaks only of a person for whose benefit the action has been brought`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 5(1): medical and funeral expenses. Arguments: date of death, the expense, incurred by a beneficiary.
-- Mia paid Daniel's hospital bill and the funeral.
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` `Daniel died` `medical expenses incurred as a result of the injury that caused the death` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: damages may be awarded for them, since they were incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought (s 5(1)); the amount is for the court`
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` `Daniel died` `the funeral expenses of the deceased` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: damages may be awarded for them, since they were incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought (s 5(1)); the amount is for the court`
-- The funeral was paid by Daniel's employer: s 5(1)'s "if" fails.
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` `Daniel died` `the funeral expenses of the deceased` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 5(1) allows them only if they were incurred by the persons for whose benefit the action is brought`
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` `Daniel died` `some other expense or loss` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `section 5(1) speaks only of medical expenses that result from the fatal injury and the funeral expenses; other losses are for the court to assess under section 6(2)`
-- The commencement edge: a death on 8 October 1959 (the day the Act came into operation) is not "after" it; 9 October is.
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` (`the day` 8 10 1959) `the funeral expenses of the deceased` TRUE EQUALS `not covered` `section 5(1) speaks only of a death after the Act came into operation on 8 October 1959`
#ASSERT `Can these expenses be recovered question` (`the day` 9 10 1959) `the funeral expenses of the deceased` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: damages may be awarded for them, since they were incurred by a person for whose benefit the action is brought (s 5(1)); the amount is for the court`

-- s 5(2), (3): benefits left out. Arguments: the benefit, finalised before 19 May 2008.
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `money paid or payable on the death under a contract of insurance (for example life insurance)` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `money paid or payable out of a superannuation, provident or like fund or scheme` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `a benefit from a friendly society, benefit society or trade union` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `a pension under the Repatriation Act 1920-1957 (Cth), or an Act amending or replacing it` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `a pension under the Social Services Consolidation Act 1947-1953 (Cth), or an Act amending or replacing it` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `a pension under the Coal Industry Superannuation Act 1989, or an Act amending or replacing it` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `a pension paid to the deceased's spouse or de facto partner under any other Act` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `damages for the deceased's own pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm or shortened life, recovered or recoverable for the estate under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 s 4(2a)` FALSE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
-- s 5(3): an action settled before 19 May 2008 keeps the old law for (d) only.
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `damages for the deceased's own pain and suffering, bodily or mental harm or shortened life, recovered or recoverable for the estate under the Law Reform (Miscellaneous Provisions) Act 1941 s 4(2a)` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `no: section 5(2)(d) does not apply, because the action or claim was finalised by judgment or agreement before 19 May 2008 (s 5(3))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `money paid or payable on the death under a contract of insurance (for example life insurance)` TRUE EQUALS `answered` `yes: it is not taken into account in assessing the damages (s 5(2))`
#ASSERT `Is this benefit left out question` `something else (for example an inheritance from the estate, or a payment section 5(2) does not list)` FALSE EQUALS `not covered` `section 5(2) does not list this benefit, so this Act does not say whether it is taken into account; that is for the general law of damages`

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- s 6(4): sharing. $500,000 recovered, $20,000 of costs not recovered from the defendant: $480,000 to divide; Mia's 60% is $288,000.
#ASSERT `How much is there to share question` 500000 20000 EQUALS 480000
#ASSERT `What is my share question` 500000 20000 60 EQUALS 288000
#ASSERT `What is my share question` 300000 0 25 EQUALS 75000
-- Costs equal to, or above, the damages: nothing to divide.
#ASSERT `How much is there to share question` 10000 10000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `How much is there to share question` 10000 15000 EQUALS 0
#ASSERT `What is my share question` 10000 15000 50 EQUALS 0

-- s 6(5)-(7): payment into court. Arguments: paid in, accepted, issue taken, court decided enough.
#ASSERT `What follows from the payment into court question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `sections 6(5)-(7) do not apply: the defendant has not paid money into court`
#ASSERT `What follows from the payment into court question` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `the money was paid in as one sum for all of us, and none of it is paid out except under a judge's order; the court divides it (s 6(5), (6))`
#ASSERT `What follows from the payment into court question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict: it applies only where the plaintiff takes issue that the money paid in is not enough`
#ASSERT `What follows from the payment into court question` TRUE FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS `the defendant is entitled to the verdict on the issue of whether the money paid in was enough (s 6(7))`
#ASSERT `What follows from the payment into court question` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `section 6(7) does not give the defendant the verdict on that issue: the court did not decide the money was enough, so the court assesses the damages (s 6(2), (7))`
